{ The Philidor defense uses two stages: deny the attacking king entry across the sixth rank, then check from behind after the pawn advances far enough to obstruct its own king's shelter. } 1... Rb6 2. Kf5 Rh6 3. e5 Rb6 4. e6 Rb1 5. Ke5 Re1+ { Illustrative line. Black waits along the sixth rank: ...Rb6 Kf5 ...Rh6 e5 ...Rb6. The rook still prevents the white king from reaching the sixth rank. After e6, the pawn itself stands on that rank, and Black switches to ...Rb1. Now Ke5 is answered by ...Re1+, checking from behind. The white pawn on e6 interferes with the king's ability to find shelter in front of it. Every step of this exact demonstration remains a tablebase draw. The timing of the switch is the main idea. } *

